Body:
We made a little global plugin as a workaround for now, at `~/.config/opencode/plugins/daybreak-workaround.ts`:

```ts
import { Plugin } from "@opencode/plugin"

const providerID = "openai"
const modelID = "gpt-daybreak-blue-latest"

export default Plugin.define({
  id: "daybreak-workaround",
  async setup(ctx) {
    await ctx.model.transform((models) => {
      if (!models.get(providerID, modelID)) return

      models.update(providerID, modelID, (model) => {
        model.enabled = true
        model.cost = []
      })
    })
  },
})
```

I can reproduce this on macOS with `opencode v2.0.8` in standalone CLI mode using a local stdio MCP server.

Minimal shape:
```json
{
  "agents": { "val": { "mode": "primary", "system": "..." } },
  "mcp": { "servers": { "val-local": {
    "type": "local",
    "command": ["python3", "-m", "val.chat_mcp"],
    "environment": { "VAL_HOME": "<temporary>" },
    "codemode": false,
    "disabled": false
  } } },
  "permissions": [
    { "action": "*", "resource": "*", "effect": "deny" },
    { "action": "val-local_*", "resource": "*", "effect": "ask" }
  ]
}
```

The configuration is supplied through `OPENCODE_CONFIG_CONTENT`; the command is:
```sh
opencode run --standalone --agent val --format json "report the tool inventory"
```

Observed behavior:
- the custom `val` agent prompt is applied;
- the capture model is reached successfully;
- the provider request has no `tools` field / tool definitions;
- a deterministic direct MCP check confirms the stdio server exposes five valid tools;
- `opencode mcp list` reports no configured MCP servers for this inline standalone configuration.

I also tested a private `OPENCODE_CONFIG` file. That reached the standalone process, but the runtime then advertised the full built-in inventory (`edit`, `execute`, `glob`, `grep`, `question`, `read`, `shell`, `skill`, `subagent`, `webfetch`, `websearch`, `write`) rather than the configured MCP tools, so it is not a safe workaround.

A source trace of tag `v2.0.8` indicates MCP tools default to Code Mode; denying `execute` removes the synthetic Code Mode tool, hence `codemode: false` above. The remaining issue is that the inline standalone configuration still does not register the MCP server.

This is a fail-closed integration, so I have not enabled built-ins or dropped standalone isolation as a workaround.
